            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "Solves a set of linear equations: given the matrix A of their coefficients and the values b they must equal, finds the unknowns x. For example, 2x + y = 3.5 and x + 3y = 3 is A = 2 1, 1 3 and b = 3.5 3.\n\nUse it to find the mix of known patterns that adds up to a reading, the weights that reach given targets, or the point where lines or planes meet. It is quicker and more accurate than multiplying b by the inverse of A.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.linalg.solve\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nA matrix:\nThe square matrix of coefficients, one row per equation. Every input solves and sends - the only inlet that makes the node send.\n\nb:\nWhat each equation must equal, held: one value per row of A, or a matrix with one column per problem to solve with the same A.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nx:\nThe unknowns. Nothing is sent if A has no inverse (its equations contradict or repeat each other); t.linalg.lstsq gives the best answer there.\n\nThe answer stays on the input's device: a tensor on the GPU is worked on there and comes out there.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\n2x + y = 3.5 and x + 3y = 3: x is 1.5 and y is 0.5 (2 x 1.5 + 0.5 = 3.5; 1.5 + 3 x 0.5 = 3).\n\nRELATED:\nnp.linalg.solve is the numpy version. t.linalg.lstsq solves when there are more equations than unknowns. t.linalg.inv gives the inverse itself.",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 760,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 1000,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "str"
                }
            },
            "text": "Solves a set of linear equations: given the matrix A of their coefficients and the values b they must equal, finds the unknowns x. For example, 2x + y = 3.5 and x + 3y = 3 is A = 2 1, 1 3 and b = 3.5 3.\n\nUse it to find the mix of known patterns that adds up to a reading, the weights that reach given targets, or the point where lines or planes meet. It is quicker and more accurate than multiplying b by the inverse of A.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.linalg.solve\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nA matrix:\nThe square matrix of coefficients, one row per equation. Every input solves and sends - the only inlet that makes the node send.\n\nb:\nWhat each equation must equal, held: one value per row of A, or a matrix with one column per problem to solve with the same A.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nx:\nThe unknowns. Nothing is sent if A has no inverse (its equations contradict or repeat each other); t.linalg.lstsq gives the best answer there.\n\nThe answer stays on the input's device: a tensor on the GPU is worked on there and comes out there.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\n2x + y = 3.5 and x + 3y = 3: x is 1.5 and y is 0.5 (2 x 1.5 + 0.5 = 3.5; 1.5 + 3 x 0.5 = 3).\n\nRELATED:\nnp.linalg.solve is the numpy version. t.linalg.lstsq solves when there are more equations than unknowns. t.linalg.inv gives the inverse itself."
        },
